1
00:00:10,476 --> 00:00:12,561
[METAL CLINKING]

2
00:00:46,302 --> 00:00:47,902
-[CHUCKLES]
-[CELL PHONE BUZZING]

3
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:49,347
Oh.

4
00:00:50,682 --> 00:00:52,224
[SIGHS] Graner.

5
00:00:52,308 --> 00:00:54,727
MR. GRANER: <i>We ran the data
from Kilmer's head like you asked.</i>

6
00:00:54,811 --> 00:00:57,396
<i>We found the signature from the
console they used to hack the chip.</i>

7
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:59,398
[EXHALES DEEPLY] Whose was it?

8
00:00:59,482 --> 00:01:00,649
<i>Ours.</i>

9
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:02,359
<i>I think it's traceable to Reghabi</i>.

10
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:04,903
-Reghabi.
-<i>Yep.</i>

11
00:01:04,988 --> 00:01:07,407
<i>She cracked reintegration. I'll find her.</i>

12
00:01:08,616 --> 00:01:10,326
[SIGHS]

13
00:01:20,628 --> 00:01:23,464
[BREATHING DEEPLY]

14
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:35,351
[INHALES SHARPLY]
"Tame in me the tempers four

15
00:01:37,145 --> 00:01:39,480
"that I may serve thee evermore."

16
00:01:41,357 --> 00:01:44,152
"Place in me the values nine"

17
00:01:45,528 --> 00:01:48,448
that I may feel thy touch divine."

18
00:03:46,022 --> 00:03:47,023
[BEEPS]

19
00:03:56,490 --> 00:03:57,700
[CELL PHONE SNAPS SHUT]

20
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:03,622
[SIGHS]

21
00:04:03,706 --> 00:04:05,708
[ENGINE STARTS]

22
00:04:11,505 --> 00:04:12,506
[SIGHS]

23
00:04:13,674 --> 00:04:16,010
[TIRES SCREECH]

24
00:04:22,974 --> 00:04:24,434
[TIRES SCREECH]

25
00:04:28,605 --> 00:04:29,731
[HAND BRAKE ENGAGES]

26
00:04:39,199 --> 00:04:40,492
[HAND BRAKE DISENGAGES]

27
00:04:48,958 --> 00:04:51,503
[ELECTRICITY HUMMING]

28
00:04:54,756 --> 00:04:56,299
IRVING: Can I come in?

29
00:04:56,383 --> 00:04:58,093
BURT: Yeah. Mm-hmm. Yeah.

30
00:05:07,936 --> 00:05:09,646
IRVING: You found this?

31
00:05:10,814 --> 00:05:12,107
BURT: A while back.

32
00:05:13,483 --> 00:05:16,861
I come here sometimes, just me.

33
00:05:21,908 --> 00:05:24,577
Now, I want to show it to you.

34
00:05:25,954 --> 00:05:27,247
It's beautiful.

35
00:05:29,332 --> 00:05:33,128
It could be just for us. Our secret place.

36
00:05:35,297 --> 00:05:36,463
Finally.

37
00:05:37,756 --> 00:05:41,718
What I mean to say is that your
O&D colleagues a-are very nice,

38
00:05:41,802 --> 00:05:43,345
but they're also, uh…

39
00:05:43,429 --> 00:05:45,306
-Always around.
-Yes.

40
00:05:53,897 --> 00:05:55,316
BURT: Is this okay?

41
00:05:56,525 --> 00:05:57,526
[SIGHS]

42
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:04,700
Irving, you know, the Lumon manual doesn't
say anything about lip-to-lip contact.

43
00:06:05,743 --> 00:06:10,413
It does discourage romantic
fraternization, though.

44
00:06:10,497 --> 00:06:12,333
This can't be romantic then.

45
00:06:14,501 --> 00:06:15,502
No.

46
00:06:17,671 --> 00:06:21,342
Not romantic… at all?

47
00:06:25,846 --> 00:06:27,138
[WHISPERS] I'm truly sorry…

48
00:06:27,222 --> 00:06:31,226
[INHALES DEEPLY] I… I'm just not ready.

49
00:06:32,061 --> 00:06:33,062
[SIGHS]

50
00:06:34,229 --> 00:06:35,606
I'm… I'm sorry.

51
00:06:38,359 --> 00:06:39,902
[SMACKS LIPS, INHALES SHARPLY]

52
00:06:41,153 --> 00:06:42,279
It's fine.

53
00:06:45,532 --> 00:06:46,700
Just stay.

54
00:06:48,911 --> 00:06:52,205
Stay here with me.

55
00:06:56,084 --> 00:06:58,336
[CHILDREN CHATTERING]

56
00:07:01,631 --> 00:07:03,841
[HUMMING]

57
00:07:13,393 --> 00:07:15,436
-You did so good.
-[WHIMPERS]

58
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:19,565
Now we're gonna go home
and see if you'll eat. Please.

59
00:07:26,948 --> 00:07:27,949
Ah!

60
00:07:31,661 --> 00:07:32,787
[CHUCKLES]

61
00:07:45,383 --> 00:07:47,635
Looks like we both made it through.

62
00:07:47,719 --> 00:07:49,637
-Oh, hello.
-Hi. [CHUCKLES]

63
00:07:50,346 --> 00:07:52,389
Devon. From the retreat.

64
00:07:52,473 --> 00:07:54,684
I accosted you for your coffee.

65
00:07:55,184 --> 00:07:56,936
-[CHUCKLES]
-[LAUGHS]

66
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:58,771
Aw, he's beautiful.

67
00:07:58,855 --> 00:08:00,148
He looks like a William.

68
00:08:00,898 --> 00:08:03,026
His name's Bradley, actually.

69
00:08:04,193 --> 00:08:05,193
Not William?

70
00:08:06,611 --> 00:08:09,573
-You changed it?
-Did we leave the juice boxes in the car?

71
00:08:10,449 --> 00:08:11,749
GABBY: We must have, yeah.

72
00:08:12,534 --> 00:08:15,328
Hi. Angelo Arteta.

73
00:08:15,412 --> 00:08:17,121
I'm Devon. Nice to meet you.

74
00:08:17,205 --> 00:08:19,582
-ANGELO: Congratulations there.
-Oh, thank you.

75
00:08:19,666 --> 00:08:21,376
You two know each other?

76
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:24,212
Yeah, I was… We were…
met at the birthing cottages.

77
00:08:24,296 --> 00:08:25,589
Mm-hmm.

78
00:08:26,256 --> 00:08:29,259
-Nice to meet you too, Devon.
-[LAUGHS] Yeah. You too.

79
00:08:32,429 --> 00:08:33,470
Wow-kazow.

80
00:08:33,554 --> 00:08:34,765
-[CHUCKLES]
-Mm-hmm.

81
00:08:35,765 --> 00:08:36,767
We should go.

82
00:08:38,684 --> 00:08:40,354
-GABBY: Bye. Bye.
-Bye, you guys.

83
00:08:41,438 --> 00:08:42,438
Bye, Bradley.

84
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:43,982
[GABBY CHUCKLES]

85
00:08:45,359 --> 00:08:47,486
ANGELO: Declan, Kai, come on.

86
00:08:48,612 --> 00:08:50,822
What in the fuckity fuck?

87
00:08:50,906 --> 00:08:51,990
[ELEANOR FUSSES]

88
00:08:52,074 --> 00:08:53,075
Sorry.

89
00:08:55,327 --> 00:08:57,412
RICKEN: <i>What does camaraderie mean?</i>

90
00:08:59,247 --> 00:09:02,584
<i>Most linguists agree it
comes from the Latin "camera,"</i>

91
00:09:03,210 --> 00:09:06,338
<i>which means "a device
used to take a photograph."</i>

92
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:12,343
<i>And of course, the best photographs
are of groups of happy friends</i>

93
00:09:12,427 --> 00:09:13,971
<i>who love each other deeply.</i>

94
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:19,392
<i>But I think camaraderie is more
than smiling together in photos.</i>

95
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:22,645
<i>It's standing together in hard times.</i>

96
00:09:23,396 --> 00:09:27,149
<i>It's recognizing a common
struggle in another person</i>

97
00:09:27,984 --> 00:09:32,071
<i>and reaching out to
offer them a loving hand.</i>

98
00:09:37,368 --> 00:09:40,329
We should return to O&D immediately.

99
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:41,914
All of us. Today.

100
00:09:41,998 --> 00:09:43,415
Someone's eager to fraternize.

101
00:09:43,499 --> 00:09:45,250
Fraternization has nothing to do with it.

102
00:09:45,334 --> 00:09:48,712
This could be the start of
us uniting the departments

103
00:09:48,796 --> 00:09:50,881
the way Kier always intended.

104
00:09:50,965 --> 00:09:52,758
Maybe his ghost can
officiate your wedding.

105
00:09:52,842 --> 00:09:55,928
IRVING: This is inappropriate workplace
commentary. And I'm self-reporting you.

106
00:09:56,012 --> 00:09:58,180
-You're reporting me to yourself?
-IRVING: Mm-hmm.

107
00:09:58,264 --> 00:10:00,140
Okay. You said they were making things?

108
00:10:00,224 --> 00:10:02,392
Yes, in some kind of machines.
We didn't ask what.

109
00:10:02,476 --> 00:10:04,186
It's the clubs they
murder those goats with.

110
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:05,271
HELLY: Shut up.

111
00:10:05,855 --> 00:10:08,065
I agree with Irving.
O&D is the next piece of this.

112
00:10:08,149 --> 00:10:12,349
-Now, once we've mapped the whole floor…
-To be clear, I do not approve of mapping.

113
00:10:12,737 --> 00:10:13,820
Right.

114
00:10:13,904 --> 00:10:16,406
What if we go on another
"mental health walk"?

115
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:17,491
Could be fun.

116
00:10:19,994 --> 00:10:21,161
What… I don't know.

117
00:10:21,245 --> 00:10:24,665
I mean, we… we…
we still have a lot of work to do, so…

118
00:10:24,749 --> 00:10:25,958
Oh, right.

119
00:10:27,335 --> 00:10:29,962
The work is mysterious and important.

120
00:10:32,214 --> 00:10:35,174
That's good.
That sounds just like me. [LAUGHS]

121
00:10:35,258 --> 00:10:36,301
I know.

122
00:10:38,637 --> 00:10:40,305
Back to work, slackers.

123
00:10:44,517 --> 00:10:45,518
Praise Kier.

124
00:10:47,145 --> 00:10:48,146
Hey.

125
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:50,357
Are you fraternizing too?

126
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:53,109
What? No, I…

127
00:10:53,193 --> 00:10:57,029
I'm a leader who cares about his employees

128
00:10:57,113 --> 00:10:58,990
and takes their requests seriously.

129
00:10:59,074 --> 00:11:00,199
-Really?
-Yeah.

130
00:11:00,283 --> 00:11:02,827
Look at you all dewy mouthed.

131
00:11:02,911 --> 00:11:04,954
-You never smile at me.
-He's right, Mark.

132
00:11:05,038 --> 00:11:08,165
You are sparing with the
facial encouragements.

133
00:11:08,249 --> 00:11:10,376
That's ridiculous. I smile all the time.

134
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:11,660
MR. MILCHICK: Hey there.

135
00:11:12,379 --> 00:11:14,171
What are you all talking about?

136
00:11:14,255 --> 00:11:15,298
MARK: Oh. Uh…

137
00:11:15,382 --> 00:11:16,924
[STAMMERS] Just, uh…

138
00:11:17,008 --> 00:11:18,009
-We're…
-Dis…

139
00:11:18,093 --> 00:11:20,428
-Discussing the…
-Yes, uh, Mrs., uh…

140
00:11:20,512 --> 00:11:23,807
-Ms. Casey. Ms. Casey.
-Ms. Casey. Yeah, mm-hmm.

141
00:11:24,975 --> 00:11:26,017
Wait, where is she?

142
00:11:28,603 --> 00:11:34,275
MS. COBEL: Part-time innies may not be as
socialized and sophisticated as yourself,

143
00:11:34,359 --> 00:11:37,779
but they still must be held
accountable for their actions.

144
00:11:38,363 --> 00:11:43,493
Ms. Casey was told to supervise
Helly, and she failed at that task.

145
00:11:44,494 --> 00:11:46,412
Because I snuck Helly out.

146
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:49,196
I mean, if anyone should
be in the break room, it's me.

147
00:11:49,415 --> 00:11:50,416
[SCOFFS]

148
00:11:51,292 --> 00:11:53,710
Ah, valiance.

149
00:11:53,794 --> 00:11:56,505
Not a core principle, but sweet.

150
00:11:57,548 --> 00:12:00,009
Who won't you go to the break room for?

151
00:12:02,428 --> 00:12:05,556
She's just a wellness counselor, Mark.

152
00:12:06,098 --> 00:12:07,891
Yes, but she shouldn't be punished.

153
00:12:07,975 --> 00:12:09,643
I mean, I thought Helly needed a walk.

154
00:12:09,727 --> 00:12:11,353
I mean, I'm allowed to do that.

155
00:12:11,437 --> 00:12:13,022
Just a walk?

156
00:12:13,647 --> 00:12:14,648
Yeah.

157
00:12:20,362 --> 00:12:22,572
Or were you sniffing
around other departments

158
00:12:22,656 --> 00:12:26,076
while your department
falls woefully below quota?

159
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:28,620
She almost died.

160
00:12:28,704 --> 00:12:32,416
It's not your job to play
nursemaid to every new refiner.

161
00:12:34,001 --> 00:12:36,294
Okay, so what is my job?

162
00:12:36,378 --> 00:12:37,963
Are you really asking me that?

163
00:12:39,381 --> 00:12:40,382
Yeah.

164
00:12:40,883 --> 00:12:43,052
What is it we actually do here?

165
00:12:45,429 --> 00:12:48,390
We serve Kier, you child!

166
00:12:48,891 --> 00:12:54,813
And until you get that through your
mildewed little brain and hit quota,

167
00:12:55,397 --> 00:12:59,360
MDR's hallway privileges
are hereby revoked.

168
00:13:00,194 --> 00:13:05,323
So get your little ass back to your desk
and stay there until you're told to move.

169
00:13:07,659 --> 00:13:08,910
[BREATHES SHAKILY]

170
00:13:12,205 --> 00:13:14,498
DYLAN: Goddamn tragedy for the ages.

171
00:13:14,582 --> 00:13:18,043
MDR will mourn the "will-they-won't-they"
energy shared by Ms. Casey and myself.

172
00:13:18,127 --> 00:13:19,128
HELLY: Oh, yeah?

173
00:13:19,212 --> 00:13:21,862
And to think I wore this
nice-ass shirt for her today.

174
00:13:23,549 --> 00:13:25,759
When you picked that shirt,
you didn't know she existed.

175
00:13:25,843 --> 00:13:28,178
Well, maybe love transcends severance.

176
00:13:28,262 --> 00:13:30,348
-You think so?
-No.

177
00:13:32,976 --> 00:13:34,226
What about you and Mark?

178
00:13:34,310 --> 00:13:35,561
[SCOFFS] What?

179
00:13:35,645 --> 00:13:38,147
You two enjoy, uh,
sneaking off the other day?

180
00:13:38,898 --> 00:13:40,274
"Baby goats"?

181
00:13:41,526 --> 00:13:43,361
Why are you saying it like that?

182
00:13:43,945 --> 00:13:44,821
[MUMBLES]

183
00:13:45,613 --> 00:13:49,413
Are you implying that "baby goats" is…
[STAMMERS] code for sex with Mark S.?

184
00:13:50,159 --> 00:13:52,203
-Mm-hmm.
-Wow.

185
00:13:53,371 --> 00:13:56,457
Is…
Is "baby goats" code for sex with Mark S.?

186
00:13:57,041 --> 00:13:58,459
No, they were actual goats.

187
00:13:58,543 --> 00:14:00,044
Why would we call it that?

188
00:14:00,753 --> 00:14:02,838
Okay. Yeah, I believe you.

189
00:14:02,922 --> 00:14:04,422
-[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]
-Mark.

190
00:14:05,925 --> 00:14:07,092
You find out what happened?

191
00:14:07,176 --> 00:14:09,220
She's in the break room.

192
00:14:09,304 --> 00:14:11,889
Shit. Is that because of us?

193
00:14:14,350 --> 00:14:17,478
And we're not allowed in the outer
hallways anymore till we hit quota,

194
00:14:17,562 --> 00:14:21,231
so no more interdepartmental visits!

195
00:14:23,942 --> 00:14:25,444
Are you serious?

196
00:14:27,237 --> 00:14:28,322
[EXHALES SHARPLY]

197
00:14:29,698 --> 00:14:31,783
[EXHALES, WHISPERS] I'm…

198
00:14:33,702 --> 00:14:36,052
[NORMAL] I'm sorry, Mark.
It's… It's my fault…

199
00:14:37,914 --> 00:14:43,253
That I've been setting a bad
example as the senior-most refiner.

200
00:14:48,967 --> 00:14:51,094
Which way did you say it was to O&D?

201
00:15:13,825 --> 00:15:15,451
MS. COBEL: Milchick!

202
00:15:15,535 --> 00:15:16,620
I'm on it.

203
00:15:38,224 --> 00:15:40,226
[MACHINES RATTLING]

204
00:15:45,564 --> 00:15:46,565
Whoa.

205
00:15:50,236 --> 00:15:52,236
This is more people than I've ever seen.

206
00:15:52,655 --> 00:15:54,073
Uh, same.

207
00:15:56,993 --> 00:15:58,452
It's okay.

208
00:15:58,536 --> 00:16:02,415
I know change can be disorienting,

209
00:16:02,999 --> 00:16:04,750
but MDR is here now.

210
00:16:07,795 --> 00:16:09,087
You are welcome here,

211
00:16:09,171 --> 00:16:14,801
much as I hope all of us will be welcome
to visit you at your place of endeavors.

212
00:16:14,885 --> 00:16:18,723
Now go on. Surely you must
have some questions for them.

213
00:16:24,312 --> 00:16:27,273
So, it's called Macrodata Refinement?

214
00:16:29,650 --> 00:16:31,277
What do you refine?

215
00:16:33,070 --> 00:16:34,155
Uh…

216
00:16:36,324 --> 00:16:37,491
Is that a watering can?

217
00:16:40,536 --> 00:16:45,290
We think it might be supplies
for the executive wing upstairs.

218
00:16:45,374 --> 00:16:49,752
Then again, last week's output
had more of an aggressive feel.

219
00:16:49,836 --> 00:16:51,212
The hatchets weren't aggressive.

220
00:16:51,296 --> 00:16:53,256
-[SHUSHES]
-Hatchets?

221
00:16:53,340 --> 00:16:54,590
MARK: Um… [STAMMERS]

222
00:16:54,674 --> 00:16:57,385
We've been trying to figure
out how it all fits together.

223
00:16:57,469 --> 00:17:01,389
We found a, uh, department
the opposite way from here

224
00:17:01,473 --> 00:17:05,268
that's, uh, well, raising baby goats.

225
00:17:06,519 --> 00:17:07,854
Raising baby goats?

226
00:17:12,692 --> 00:17:17,572
There is a lot unknown to us as
well, but we keep plugging along.

227
00:17:17,656 --> 00:17:19,323
It's important work, obviously.

228
00:17:19,407 --> 00:17:22,034
Everything we do here is important.

229
00:17:24,329 --> 00:17:27,729
It's important because it actually
is or because you're saying it is?

230
00:17:30,168 --> 00:17:33,755
Look, maybe we should
work together on this.

231
00:17:35,632 --> 00:17:39,260
Together doing what, exactly?

232
00:17:39,344 --> 00:17:42,430
I don't know.
Finding out why there are goats.

233
00:17:42,514 --> 00:17:47,310
Or, you know, um, finding
out how big this place is

234
00:17:47,394 --> 00:17:49,062
or how many of us there are.

235
00:17:54,275 --> 00:17:57,862
I mean, why won't they tell
us what we're doing here?

236
00:17:58,822 --> 00:18:00,532
What-What are they so afraid of?

237
00:18:02,282 --> 00:18:06,661
If the Eagan philosophy
is illumination above all…

238
00:18:06,745 --> 00:18:08,622
Illumination beyond all.

239
00:18:08,706 --> 00:18:09,748
But yes.

240
00:18:10,499 --> 00:18:12,960
Then why doesn't that include us?

241
00:18:13,669 --> 00:18:18,632
Why are we down here
still working in the dark?

242
00:18:25,639 --> 00:18:27,432
That was poetic as shit, man.

243
00:18:27,516 --> 00:18:28,600
Thanks.

244
00:18:31,145 --> 00:18:32,271
Mark is right.

245
00:18:32,896 --> 00:18:34,439
-He is?
-BURT: He is.

246
00:18:34,523 --> 00:18:40,528
Irving, Kier would want us to feel the
warm embrace of knowledge and truth.

247
00:18:40,612 --> 00:18:43,532
That way we could be true
partners in his teachings.

248
00:18:44,116 --> 00:18:45,117
Exactly.

249
00:18:46,577 --> 00:18:50,873
I think, as the two department chiefs,

250
00:18:51,665 --> 00:18:55,836
Mark and I should make contact with
this goat department, see what they know.

251
00:18:57,588 --> 00:19:02,051
-And we can each bring one aide-de-camp.
-[DOOR OPENS]

252
